    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:1",
      "id": "lem-universally-closed-valuative-existence-quasicompact",
      "location": "Proof 3.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof concludes, “Therefore every nonzero prime avoiding t equals P,” after establishing only that such a prime contains P and avoids t. This does not itself exclude P strictly contained in the prime strictly contained in m. In this construction, if r is in such a prime outside P, then v(r)=(0,q) for rational q>0; choosing N with 0<Nq<1 gives t/r^N in R and t=r^N(t/r^N), forcing t into the prime, a contradiction. That short classification step is needed for the two cases used in Proof 4.1.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
